# Intermediate phase-enhanced Ostwald ripening for the elimination of phase segregation in efficient inorganic CsPbIBr2 perovskite solar cells

ABSTRACT Mixed halide perovskites with the ability to tune bandgaps exhibit attractive applications in tandem solar cells, building integrated photovoltaic and wavelength-tunable light-emitting devices. However, halide demixing under illumination or in the dark with a charge-carrier injection in both hybrid and inorganic perovskites results in bandgap instability and current-density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ hysteresis, which can significantly hamper their application. Here, we demonstrate that halide segregation and J-V hysteresis in mixed halide inorganic $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ solar cells can be effectively mitigated by introducing an intermediate phase-enhanced Ostwald ripening through the control of the chemical composition in the $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ precursor solution. Excess amounts of either $\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ or CsI are incorporated into originally even molar amounts of $\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ and CsI precursor solutions. With the $\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ -excess, we observed an enlarged perovskite grain size, no detectable halide phase segregation at the grain boundaries nor the perovskite $\mathbf{\nabla}^{\prime}\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ interface, an increased minority carrier lifetime, a reduced $J{-}V$ hysteresis, and an improved solar-cell performance. However, different $\mathbf{CsI}{\mathbf{:Pb}}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ stoichiometric ratios were found to have different effects on the performance of the perovskite solar cell. The excessive lead phase is reactive with the dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) in the precursor solution to form the $\mathbf{Pb}(\mathbf{I},\mathbf{Br})_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}$ complex and the quasi-twodimensional (2D) $\mathbf{CsPb}_{2}(\mathbf{I},\ \mathbf{Br})_{5},$ which are conducive to Ostwald maturation and defect extinction. Finally, the $\mathbf{Cs}\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{IB}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ solar cell with a $\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ -excess precursor composition reaches a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $9.37\%$ (stabilized PCE of $\mathbf{8.48\%}$ ) and a maximum external quantum efficiency of over $90\%$ .
Keywords: solar cells, phase segregation, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2},$ cathodoluminescence, transmission electron microscope
## INTRODUCTION
Photoactive materials with the perovskite structure show great potential in the next-generation photovoltaics and optoelectronics. In particular, the ability to tune bandgaps with adjustments of the composition is one of the most attractive properties for their potential applications in tandem solar cells [1], building integrated photovoltaics [2], and wavelength-tunable light-emitting devices (LEDs) [3]. The bandgap of perovskite absorbers can be continuously tuned from approximately 1.2 to $2.3\:\mathrm{eV}$ by varying the composition of the $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ formula, with A cations, such as methylammonium $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{2}}^{+})$ , formamidinium $\mathrm{(CH(NH_{2})_{2}^{+})}$ , and cesium $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}\boldsymbol{s}^{+})$ ; B cations such as tin $(\mathsf{S n}^{2+})$ and lead $({\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+})$ ; and halides, such as iodide $\bar{(\mathrm{I~})}$ , bromide $\mathrm{(Br^{-})}$ , and chloride $\bar{(\mathrm{Cl}^{-})}$ , as the X anion [4,5]. However, both hybrid and inorganic perovskites with a large bandgap are subject to bandgap instability due to halide demixing under illumination or in the dark with charge-carrier injection [6–9]. Halide segregation can prevent the achievement of spectrally stable LED devices [10] and, also, potentially enhance ion migration and alter the electric-field gradients within the solar cell, negatively influencing the performance and stability of the device [8,11–14].
Halide segregation within an initially perfect single crystal, such as $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x},$ is not energetically favorable as a large lattice mismatch exists between the iodide-rich and bromide-rich phases [15]. However, near a defect, such as a grain boundary (GB), the lattice mismatch may be more easily accommodated with a lower energy penalty. Furthermore, the formation energy of the point defects at the GBs, and the film’s surfaces can be much lower than that in the grain interiors [16,17]. In our previous study, cathodoluminescence (CL) was employed to further reveal the nanoscale halide phase segregation in inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ , in which the iodide-rich $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ phases were identified to segregate at the GBs [8]. The crystalline defects, typically halide vacancies and interstitials, promoted halide migration and segregation by providing low-energy migration pathways that could potentially lead to current-density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ hysteresis [17–19]. Furthermore, a free carrier trapped at the defective GBs with a low bandgap, such as the iodiderich phase of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x},$ could greatly increase the recombination rate [20].
Therefore, it is important to understand the relationship between the phase segregation and the crystalline defects to further improve the performance and stability of the perovskite solar cells (PSCs). The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3-x}}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ inorganic perovskites exhibit better structural stability than the hybrid perovskites under electron beams, enabling analysis of the relationship between the phase segregation and the defects’ details by advanced electron microscopy techniques [17,21–24]. Furthermore, $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ inorganic perovskites are more thermally stable than hybrid perovskites; thus, they have attracted increasing amounts of attention for their photovoltaic and light-emitting properties [7,25–33].
In this paper, we attempt to improve the quality of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ crystal to suppress the halide segregation to achieve a photo-stable material [15,18]. Excess amounts of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ or CsI were incorporated into the inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2},$ prepared from originally even molar amounts of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and CsI. We found that the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess composition favored the formation of ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}$ ·dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), and quasi-two-dimensional (2D) $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ . These intermediate phases greatly enhanced the Ostwald ripening, reducing the interface energy and increasing the mass transportation of the constituents for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystal growth [34,35]. A significantly larger grain size and a lower defect density were achieved by compositional engineering via the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ -excess composition. Therefore, no halide phase segregation was detected at the GBs nor the perovskite $\mathrm{\DeltaTiO}_{2}$ interfaces, and there was an increased minority carrier lifetime, a reduced $J{-}V$ hysteresis, and an improved solar-cell performance. In contrast, the CsI-excess did not improve the properties of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSCs in any way.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Materials
Unless specified otherwise, all the materials were purchased from either Alfa Aesar or Sigma-Aldrich and used as received. Spiro-OMeTAD (2,20,7,70-tetrakis (N,N-di$P$ -methoxyphenylamine)-9,9-spirobifluorene) was purchased from Luminescence Technology Corp.
## Device fabrication
A dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer was first deposited on a clean fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass by spray pyrolysis at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ using a bis(isopropoxide)-bis(acetylacetonate)titanium(IV) solution. After cooling to room temperature, the substrate was cut into smaller pieces of approximately 1 cm2.
## CL characterization
CL characterization was performed using an FEI Nova NanoSEM 450 scanning electron microscope (SEM) equipped with a Delmic SPARC CL System. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films for CL characterizations were deposited on a typical substrate used for photovoltaic devices [24], consisting of layers of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , FTO, and glass. Before the CL characterization, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ films were kept in a dark vacuum chamber for more than $10~\mathrm{min}$ to avoid unintended light-induced phase segregation. All of the CL mappings were recorded from the previously unexposed regions of the sample, except when specifically indicated. The CL measurements were carried out under electronbeam-accelerating voltages of 2 and $5\mathrm{kV}$ . A fresh crosssection was created by direct mechanical cleaving just before loading the sample into the SEM chamber for cross-sectional CL mapping.
At the electron-beam-acceleration voltage of $2\mathrm{kV}$ , the CL intensity mapping through the photomultiplier tube (PMT) was obtained with a resolution of $1024\times1024$ pixels ( ${\sim}11~\mathrm{nm}$ on each side) and a dwell time of $300~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ per pixel, while the CL spectral maps were obtained with a dwell time of $200~\mathrm{{ms}}$ per pixel. The electron-beam current was fixed at $440\ \mathrm{pA}$ .
At the electron-beam-acceleration voltage of $5\mathrm{kV}$ , the CL-PMT maps were obtained with a resolution of $1024\times$ 1024 pixels ( ${\sim}11~\mathrm{nm}$ on each side) and a dwell time of $50~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ per pixel, while the CL spectral maps were obtained with a dwell time of $10~\mathrm{ms}$ . The electron-beam current was fixed at 799 pA.
## Photoluminescence
The photoluminescence (PL) spectra were collected by a confocal microscope system (WITec, alpha 300R) in ambient conditions. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ samples were excited using a $532\mathrm{-nm}$ laser source. The time-resolved PL (TRPL) decay traces were measured using a time-correlated single photon counting (TCSPC) technique on a Micro Time 200 (Picoquant) microscope, by $470\mathrm{-nm}$ laser excitation at a $1\mathrm{MHz}$ repetition rate and detection through a bandpass pass filter $(582\pm75\mathrm{{nm})}$ . The excitation power density was $300\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The PL decay traces measured by the TCSPC were well fitted with a biexponential function:
$$
I(t){=}A_{1}\mathrm{exp}\biggl(-\frac{t}{\tau_{1}}\biggr)+A_{2}\mathrm{exp}\biggl(-\frac{t}{\tau_{2}}\biggr),
$$
where $I(t)$ was the time-dependent PL intensity, $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ were the lifetimes of the fast and the slow decay components, respectively, and $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ were the corresponding amplitudes. From this, we defined the effective lifetime as [36]
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{eff}}=\left(A_{1}\tau_{1}{+}A_{2}\tau_{2}\right)/\left(A_{1}{+}A_{2}\right).
$$
## Solar-cell performance characterizations
Current-voltage characterization was performed using a solar simulator (Oriel) fitted with a filtered $1000\mathrm{W}$ xenon lamp to replicate AM 1.5, $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ conditions. The illumination of the light source was calibrated using a silicon reference cell (Peccell Technologies) with a color filter to minimize the spectral mismatch between the calibration diode and the PSC. The $J{-}V$ response was recorded using a Keithley 2400 source meter. The solar cells were masked with a non-reflective metal aperture of $0.16~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ that defined the active area of the device. The $J.$ $V$ measurements were carried out with a scan rate of $100\ \mathrm{mV\s}^{-1}$ from the forward bias to the short-circuit (reverse scan) and from the short-circuit to the forward bias (forward scan). The external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements were performed using a $150\mathrm{W}$ xenon lamp (Oriel) fitted with a monochromator (Cornerstone 260). The EQE photocurrents were recorded under the short-circuit conditions using a Keithley 2400 source meter. The monochromatic photon flux was quantified via a calibrated silicon cell (Peccell Technologies).
## Other characterizations
The perovskite samples for X-ray diffraction (XRD) were prepared on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -coated FTO-glass substrates and analyzed using a D2 PHASER (Bruker Corp.). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were recorded with an Agilent Technologies Cary 630 spectrometer. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ samples were deposited by spin-coating onto glass substrates and were scratched for analysis.
## RESULTS
## Crystal growth versus composition
Chemical composition engineering has already exhibited a significant influence on crystal growth, defect passivation, and solar-cell performance in hybrid perovskites [37,38]. Stoichiometric hybrid organic-inorganic perovskites can be achieved from a non-stoichiometric precursor solution since the excess organic cations are removed during annealing due to their volatile nature [38]. In contrast, due to the superior thermal stability of the inorganic components, excess $\mathrm{CsX}$ $\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{I}$ , Br) or $\mathrm{PbX}_{2}$ are retained after annealing by the formation of secondary phases such as $\mathrm{Cs_{4}P b X_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{5},$ according to the phase diagram of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -CsBr [39]. Compared with the hybrid perovskites, this more complicated phase diagram provides improved prospects for using compositional engineering to control the crystal growth process and, thereby, to improve the generation, transportation, and collection of free carriers, as well as the recombination in inorganic perovskites. Therefore, we first studied the effect of the precursor chemical composition on the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystal growth and the microstructure evolution during thermal annealing. (Hereafter, $^{\alpha}{\mathrm{CsI}}$ -excess ” and ${}^{\mathfrak{e}}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess” refer to excess of CsI or $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}.$ , respectively, being incorporated into originally even molar amounts of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and CsI precursor solution.)
The conversion from the precursor to the crystallized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film was monitored morphologically, compositionally, and crystallographically by SEM, FTIR, and insitu heating XRD, as shown in Fig. 1. All three compositions showed almost identical average grain sizes $(<100\ \mathrm{nm})$ , after annealing at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The in-situ heating and $e x$ -situ XRD spectra in Fig. 1a and Fig. S1 prove that these small grains were mainly the “black phase” orthorhombic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ perovskite with XRD peaks at $15^{\circ}$ , $21.2^{\circ}$ , and $30.1^{\circ};$ , corresponding to the {110}, $\{200\}$ , and $\{220\}$ planes [29]. The FTIR spectra for all three compositions indicated the existence of ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}$ · DMSO, showing a peak at $\sim900{-}1000\ c m^{-1}$ that corresponded to the $s{=}0$ stretching vibration of the $\mathrm{{Pb}(I,}$ $\operatorname{Br})_{2}{\cdot}\operatorname{DMSO}$ adduct, as shown in Fig. 1b [40–42].
As the annealing temperature was increased to $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and was maintained as such for $5\mathrm{min}$ , the average grain size of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ increased to a few hundred nanometers for all three precursor compositions. The large $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystals were grown at the expense of smaller ones, consistent with Ostwald ripening [34,35] (Ostwald ripening is a phenomenon observed in solid solutions or liquid sols that describes the change of an inhomogeneous structure over time, i.e., small crystals or sol particles dissolve and are redeposited onto larger crystals or sol particles). Ostwald ripening is driven by the reduction in the total surface free energy resulting from the enlarging grain size. It has been reported that DMSO released from the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}$ adduct in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},$ $\left.\operatorname{Br}\right)_{3}$ precursor film can greatly facilitate mass transport, enhancing the Ostwald ripening and defect annihilation in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{3}$ [43,44] through the following reaction: $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}\cdot\mathrm{DMSO}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}+\mathrm{DMSO}.$ (3)
However, as the annealing time was extended to $20\mathrm{min}$ (at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ), the crystal growth of the CsI-excess samples almost finished, while the stochiometric and $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess samples kept growing. This was consistent with the fact that DMSO had been fully removed in the CsI-excess sample, while traces of DMSO could still be seen in the other two compositions after annealing at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 1b).
After annealing at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , DMSO had been fully removed from all these three compositions, as suggested in Fig. 1b. Therefore, DMSO-assisted sintering could not explain the further crystal growth of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ in the stochiometric and the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess samples. In the stochiometric and the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess samples, it was evident that the small crystals dispersed at the GBs had gradually merged into the growing $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ large crystals with increasing temperature or time (Fig. 1a). This indicated that the growth of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystals might have been further enhanced by these small crystals after the DMSO were fully evaporated. The CL spectra proved the existence of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ in these small crystals, which showed a much lower CL intensity compared with the large $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystals in Figs S2 and S3.
As the annealing continued, the residual small crystals migrated together and combined into larger crystals, which showed much darker secondary electron (SE) contrast in the SEM images collected at an acceleration voltage of $2\mathrm{kV}$ (Fig. 1a). In Fig. 2a, the energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) results prove that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ grains (grains labeled as 1–3) showed much darker SE contrast than the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{3}$ grains (grains labeled as $\llangle-$ 6). However, as the acceleration voltage for the SEM image increased from 2 to $10\mathrm{kV}$ , the contrast of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3}$ grains became almost the same. Therefore, SEM at the low-acceleration voltage $(2\mathrm{kV})$ is an effective way to identify the distribution of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ secondary phases. The existence of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{{CsPb}}_{2}(\mathrm{I},}\end{array}$ $\operatorname{Br})_{5}$ was also identified by the in-situ heating XRD with a peak position at $11.3^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the $(001)_{\mathrm{t}}$ orientation of the tetragonal $\mathrm{CsPb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ phase [45] (throughout this manuscript, the subscripts $\"\mathrm{\Delta}\mathfrak{t}^{\flat}$ and $^{\alpha}{\bf o}^{\gamma}$ denote indexing in the tetragonal and orthorhombic phases, respectively). The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ phase was formed due to the following reaction:
$$
\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}+\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{3}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}.
$$
As the annealing temperature increased to $240^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{;}}$ the ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},{\mathrm{Br}})_{2}$ was fully converted to the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ phase, as proved by the in-situ heating XRD analysis. However, after annealing at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ , the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ phase in the stochiometric sample completely disappeared in both the SEM and the in-situ heating XRD. In contrast, in the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess samples, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ phase was still present. From the observation above, it can be seen that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ and the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3}$ were mutually convertible. The small $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{3}$ crystals were first converted to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ when the excess ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},{\mathrm{Br}})_{2}$ was available. As the annealing temperature further increased, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}$ reacted with the remaining Cs (I, Br) to form $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3} $ , according to the following reaction:
$$
\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{5}+\mathrm{Cs}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})\to2\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{3}.
$$
## Phase segregation versus composition
PL is a sensitive tag, identifying the phase segregation with the formation of a strong red-shifted PL peak that was first reported by Hoke et al. [6]. Light-induced phase segregation was observed in the stoichiometric and CsIexcess $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ samples, in which the PL spectra of these perovskites developed a new, red-shifted peak at $640{-}680~\mathrm{nm}$ under constant 1-sun illumination in $^{100\ s,}$ as shown in Fig. S4a. This suggested that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ segregated into two phases: an iodide-rich minority domain $(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x})$ and a bromide-rich majority domain $(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1-x}\mathrm{Br}_{2+x})$ [31]. In contrast, the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess sample showed much better stability under continuous laser exposure with the single PL peak remaining at ${\sim}585\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the original $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ phase;
Cross-sectional CL mapping was employed to further reveal the influence of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interfacial defects on the phase segregation; this is not a well-studied phenomenon. We present CL maps of cross-sections of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films with stoichiometric, CsI-excess, and $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess precursor compositions, as shown in Fig. 3. The layered structure sequences in all these cross-sectional figures are the same as Fig. 3a (layers from top to bottom: $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}/\mathrm{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ transparent conducting oxide (TCO)). In both the stoichiometric and CsI-excess films, phase segregation took place not only at the film’s surface but also throughout the whole depth of the film. More importantly, the iodide-rich phases tended to segregate at the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C s P b I B r_{2}}$ interface, which could only be directly observed by the CL cross-sectional mapping. Even within the same single crystal, the emission from the iodide-rich phase increased from the film’s surface to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ interface (from Region 1 to Region 3 in Fig. 3a, c), indicating that an increasing amount of the iodide-rich phase was segregated at the interface. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ with an excess of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ showed a more uniform emission-peak wavelength, indicating that the phase segregation was inhibited across the depth of the film, as shown in Fig. 3c. However, the CL spectra from a series of regions adjacent to the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C s P b I B r_{2}}$ interface showed the CL peak position slightly shifting to longer wavelengths, suggesting a slightly higher iodine concentration at the interface. To summarize, the iodide-rich phases tended to segregate at the defects, such as the GBs and the heterojunction interfaces. Phase segregation can be greatly inhibited by adjusting the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ precursor solution composition with a slight $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ excess.
## $J{-}V$ hysteresis versus composition
It was also observed that the precursor chemical composition had a significant influence on the performance of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ inorganic PSCs, as shown in Fig. 4. In the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ prepared with CsI-excess, a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $7.70\%$ was achieved in a reverse $J{-}V$ scan, while a much-reduced efficiency of $4.02\%$ was derived when scanned from the short-circuit to the forward bias, as shown in Table 1. It was clear that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSCs prepared with CsI-excess exhibited a strong hysteresis in the $J{-}V$ scan measurements, with a hysteresis index (HI) of $33\%$ , as defined by Equation (1) [46]:
$$
\mathrm{HI}=\frac{\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{reverse}}-\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{forward}}}{\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{reverse}}}\times100\%.
$$
However, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSC prepared with $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess exhibited a much lower HI of $10.9\%$ , with a PCE of $7.35\%$ from the reverse $J{-}V$ scan and $6.55\%$ from the forward $J{-}V$ scan. The average HI of the $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{2}$ -excess PSCs $(19.2\%)$ was also much lower than that of the solar cells prepared with stoichiometric $(46.5\%)$ and with the CsI-excess $(42.7\%)$ precursor compositions, as shown in
Table S1. Therefore, the hysteresis of the inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ PSCs was greatly reduced by the introduction of the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess.
By increasing the precursor’s molar concentration from 0.7 to $\mathrm{1mol\bar{L}^{\mathrm{-1}}}$ , the film thickness increased from ${\sim}300$ to ${\sim}380\mathrm{nm}$ , which resulted in the average short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of all three compositions increasing from ${\sim}9$ to ${\sim}10~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , as shown in Table 1. Meanwhile, the average open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ also increased, stemming from a reduced pin-hole density, as shown in Fig. S6. However, the precursor composition did not influence the $J{-}V$ hysteresis of the PSCs.
Consequently, the PSC with the precursor’s molar concentration of $\boldsymbol{1}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and the composition of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ - excess showed the highest PCE of $9.37\%$ in a reverse $J{-}V$ scan, while a PCE of $7.94\%$ was measured from the forward $J{-}V$ scan, as seen in Table 1, resulting in a stabilized PCE of $8.48\%$ , as shown in Fig. S7a. It also showed a very broad EQE plateau of over $90\%$ between 400 and $570~\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Fig. S7b. The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ value obtained by integrating the overall EQE agreed well with that seen in the $J{-}V$ curve.
## DISCUSSION
From the above results, it is clear that a higher $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}{:}\mathrm{CsI}$ ratio is advantageous for the formation of ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}$ · DMSO and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ intermediate phases, greatly enhancing the Ostwald ripening of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3}$ [47]. Consequently, the average grain sizes of the stoichiometric, $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess, and CsI-excess compositions were ${\sim}0.6,\ {\sim}0.3$ , and ${\sim}1.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively. The intermediatephase-enhanced Ostwald ripening of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3}$ could be divided into two phases.
At the low temperature $(\leq175^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the Ostwald ripening of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{3}$ was dominantly accelerated by the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}$ adduct that was formed as a result of the coordination of the Lewis acid ${\mathrm{Pb}}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}$ and the Lewis bases DMSO with lone-pair electrons [48]. However, the iodine atom in the CsI contained lone-pair electrons that were ready to couple with the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br})_{2}$ by the formation of a more thermally stable $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ phase than the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}$ adduct [49]. Therefore, increasing the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}{:}\mathrm{CsI}$ ratio in the precursor solution resulted in the formation of a larger amount of $\mathrm{{Pb}(I,}$ $\operatorname{Br})_{2}$ ·DMSO adduct, which retarded the release of DMSO. With a higher $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ : CsI ratio, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ crystal growth and defect annihilation period were greatly elongated, and a significantly larger $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ grain size could be achieved by the trace amount of DMSO released at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
the $110_{\mathrm{o}}$ direction exhibited greater structural similarity than both the experimental and theoretical simulations, as shown in Fig. $5\mathrm{a-g}$ . In particular, these two phases shared almost identical crystal structures during annealing at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ since all the circulated spots completely disappeared when the orthorhombic $110_{\mathrm o}$ -oriented $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ phase converted to a tetragonal or cubic structure [52]. The Ostwald ripening of the $110_{\mathrm{o}}$ -oriented $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ phase was greatly enhanced by means of a quasi-2D 001t-oriented $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{I},\ \mathrm{Br})_{5}$ intermediate phase to achieve a large lateral grain size. Consequently, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ exhibited a preferred- $110_{\mathrm{o}}$ orientation, as shown in both the XRD and the SAED patterns in Figs 1c and 5d, respectively.
Therefore, the intermediate phases introduced by the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ -excess composition greatly enhanced the Ostwald ripening of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}.$ facilitating the crystal growth and defect annihilation. Therefore, the halide segregation was eliminated, the minority carrier lifetime was improved (Fig. S4b and Table S2), and the $J{-}V$ hysteresis of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSC was reduced.
